WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:08.751
FAYE MOSS: Hello! Welcome back to Superstars in History —the talk show that features famous people from

2
00:00:08.752 --> 00:00:17.954
the past. I'm your host, Faye Moss, and I've had a dream about interviewing today's guest ever since I launched this show.

3
00:00:17.955 --> 00:00:28.395
A pastor, civil rights leader, and powerful speaker, this man changed history with peaceful protests and stirring speeches. Please

4
00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:51.710
join me in welcoming a personal hero . . . Martin Luther King Jr.! MARTIN LUTHER

5
00:00:51.711 --> 00:00:53.284
KING JR.: Thank

6
00:00:53.285 --> 00:01:02.230
you, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Let's get started, Dr. King! You were born on January 15, 1929,

7
00:01:02.231 --> 00:01:04.669
in Atlanta, Georgia. Did you have a

8
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:09.808
happy childhood? MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Indeed I did, thanks to two marvelous parents who

9
00:01:09.809 --> 00:01:21.359
filled our home with love. My father was a pastor at Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta, so I grew up listening to his sermons, witnessing firsthand the power of words to move

10
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.433
and inspire people. Meanwhile my mother taught me that everyone is worthy; nobody is inferior to

11
00:01:27.434 --> 00:01:33.501
anyone else. FAYE MOSS: Those were probably important lessons, since you grew up in

12
00:01:33.502 --> 00:01:36.915
the segregated South! MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Yes, it wasn't an easy place for

13
00:01:36.916 --> 00:01:45.772
a black child to grow up. Slavery was abolished when the Civil War ended in 1865, but African Americans still weren't treated

14
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:52.137
equally, especially in the South. There were laws that made all kinds of racial discrimination legal,

15
00:01:52.138 --> 00:02:02.276
and segregation was everywhere: restaurants, movie theaters, beaches, parks . . . I couldn't swim in a city pool or drink from certain

16
00:02:02.277 --> 00:02:11.280
water fountains. When I was six years old, a white playmate I had known forever suddenly wouldn't play with me anymore because he was starting at a white school.

17
00:02:11.281 --> 00:02:15.952
Not even children's schools were outside the bondage of segregation! FAYE

18
00:02:15.953 --> 00:02:21.763
MOSS: When you were 14, you won a prize for writing a speech about segregation.

19
00:02:21.764 --> 00:02:27.754
MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Yes, I'm proud of that award. But the occasion was marred by what happened on

20
00:02:27.755 --> 00:02:36.047
the trip home. The bus driver made my teacher and me give up our seats to white passengers—we had to stand in the aisle for ninety miles!

21
00:02:36.048 --> 00:02:40.253
That was the angriest I ever was during my lifetime!

22
00:02:40.254 --> 00:02:46.033
FAYE MOSS: How awful! Did you go to college after high

23
00:02:46.034 --> 00:02:50.741
school? MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: I graduated high school at 15 and then

24
00:02:50.742 --> 00:02:58.653
attended a black college. I decided to become a minister like my father, so I went to a seminary in the North after college and eventually earned

25
00:02:58.654 --> 00:03:09.143
a Ph.D. During my studies I became fascinated by Mohandas Gandhi, the activist in India who advocated nonviolent protest as a way to reform

26
00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:15.847
society. I began incorporating Gandhi's ideas into my approach to fighting for civil rights.

27
00:03:15.848 --> 00:03:20.024
FAYE MOSS: In 1954 you moved to Alabama with your

28
00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:27.041
wife. After living in the integrated North, why did you want to return to the segregated South?

29
00:03:27.042 --> 00:03:36.224
MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Well, I was hired to serve as the pastor at Dexter Avenue Baptist Church in Montgomery, and I had a hope I could fight segregation in

30
00:03:36.225 --> 00:03:40.915
my position. The following year I did indeed help to lead a large protest in

31
00:03:40.916 --> 00:03:45.569
Montgomery. FAYE MOSS: Oh! You're talking about the Montgomery

32
00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:56.018
Bus Boycott! After Rosa Parks was arrested for refusing to give up her seat to a white person, African Americans boycotted the city buses for over a year!

33
00:03:56.019 --> 00:04:00.660
MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: We met at my church to organize the boycott, and I served

34
00:04:00.661 --> 00:04:05.897
as a spokesperson. It was exciting to be involved in the movement but

35
00:04:05.898 --> 00:04:12.116
also dangerous. I received death threats, and one night while I was out at a meeting, my home was

36
00:04:12.117 --> 00:04:15.639
bombed. FAYE MOSS: Oh no!

37
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:26.668
Was anyone hurt? MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Fortunately, no. In November 1956 the boycott finally ended when the Supreme Court ruled that

38
00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:30.233
segregated bus laws were unconstitutional. We'd won a victory, Faye, but there was more

39
00:04:30.234 --> 00:04:37.509
work to be done. I traveled around the country supporting folks in other boycotts and speaking

40
00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:44.023
about nonviolent protest. FAYE MOSS: Speaking of nonviolent protest, you participated in many

41
00:04:44.024 --> 00:04:46.246
sit-ins at lunch counters. What can you

42
00:04:46.247 --> 00:04:53.146
tell us about them? MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: In the sixties students began holding sit-ins at lunch

43
00:04:53.147 --> 00:04:57.434
counters where only white people were served. They'd

44
00:04:57.435 --> 00:05:03.463
sit down and quietly demand service. White people sometimes threw food or taunted them, but the protestors

45
00:05:03.464 --> 00:05:08.638
just sat there in silence. Those students were a

46
00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.979
glowing example of nonviolent protest. FAYE MOSS: You got arrested

47
00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:19.922
at a sit-in or march, right? MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Yes, by then I had moved back to Atlanta

48
00:05:19.923 --> 00:05:27.494
to serve as a co-pastor at Ebenezer Baptist Church. In 1963 I traveled to Birmingham, Alabama, where

49
00:05:27.495 --> 00:05:34.511
black folks were staging sit-ins and marches. When I was arrested after joining a protest, I wrote a letter from my jail cell

50
00:05:34.512 --> 00:05:39.271
that described our philosophy of nonviolence. I felt I had to write the letter—white

51
00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:47.458
ministers were calling us lawbreakers. I wanted to explain that nonviolence is a powerful and just weapon—it

52
00:05:47.459 --> 00:05:51.029
draws attention to injustices. FAYE MOSS: Your letter

53
00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:59.634
from jail is really famous. But it's probably not as famous as your "I Have a Dream" speech! When did

54
00:05:59.635 --> 00:06:09.390
you make that speech? MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Back in August 1963, I helped to organize the March for Jobs and Freedom, which attracted over

55
00:06:09.391 --> 00:06:13.934
a quarter-million people to Washington, DC. We were marching for equality for all people,

56
00:06:13.935 --> 00:06:16.726
regardless of race or gender. Most folks remember

57
00:06:16.727 --> 00:06:26.848
this part of my speech: I have a dream that my four little children will one day live in a nation where they will not be judged by the color

58
00:06:26.849 --> 00:06:32.380
of their skin— FAYE MOSS: . . . but by the

59
00:06:32.381 --> 00:06:37.911
content of their character ! I practically know the speech by heart! It

60
00:06:37.912 --> 00:06:46.276
made you super famous. You were named Time magazine's Man of the Year, and the following year, you won the

61
00:06:46.277 --> 00:06:57.099
Nobel Peace Prize. MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Yes, and the March on Washington led the government to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964, which made segregation

62
00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.386
illegal in the United States. After that I focused on international

63
00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:10.231
peace and economic justice. I spoke out about the Vietnam War and worked with the Poor People's Campaign, a coalition of poor Americans advocating

64
00:07:10.232 --> 00:07:20.435
for economic change. My final fight for justice came in 1968 when I traveled to Memphis, Tennessee, to march with sanitation workers striking for better

65
00:07:20.436 --> 00:07:25.793
pay. Perhaps you could share the rest of that story without me?

66
00:07:25.794 --> 00:07:33.526
FAYE MOSS: Of course, Dr. King. Thank you for joining us—we'll let you return to history

67
00:07:33.527 --> 00:07:41.286
now! MARTIN LUTHER KING JR.: Remember the

68
00:07:41.287 --> 00:07:50.063
power of peaceful protest! Good-bye! FAYE MOSS: On April 4, 1968, during that trip to Memphis, Dr. King stepped out onto

69
00:07:50.064 --> 00:07:56.771
the balcony of his hotel. Tragically he was assassinated by

70
00:07:56.772 --> 00:08:06.184
a man who supported segregation. Dr. King was only 39 years old when he died, but he had already accomplished so many things in his fight

71
00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:16.060
for civil rights. Today his dream of equality for all people lives on, both in his famous speeches and in civil rights movements around the

72
00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:27.881
world. Thank you for joining me. I'll be back soon to interview another superstar from the past!

